# TryHackMe: SOC Workbooks and Lookup

## Overview
This room covers how SOC analysts use workbooks and lookup tables to add context to raw alert data.
On their own, things like IP  address or file hashes tell you very little. Lookups turn them into something meaningful

## Scenario
The room walked through building and using lookup tables to enrich alert data during an investigation, showing how raw log
fields become actionable once cross-referenced against reference data. 

## My Approach
1.Identified the raw fields that needed context 
2. Built a lookup table to map the raw value to something meaningful (assest owner, reputation status, departments e.t.c)
3. Applied the lookup during the investigation to speed up traige, instead of manually researching each value, the enrichment surfaced 
the context immediately.
4. Used the enriched data to make a faster, more confident traige escalation

## Key Skills Demonstrated
- Data enrichment techniques
- Understanding how looktup tables integrate into a SIEM/SOC workflow
- Speeding up triage using pre-built reference data
- Recognizing why raw alert data alone is often insufficient for a decision

## Lesson Learned
Before this room, I underestimated how much of triage speed comes from infrastructure (good lookup tables)
rather than analyst skill alone. A well built lookup table cna turn a 20-minutes investigation into a 2-minute
one, that's a huge realization for how SOC teams actually scale.

## Reflections
This connects directly to real SOC work: analyst don't have time to manually research every IP or hash in every alert.
Lookup tables and workbooks are the quiet infrastructure that makes high alert volumes managable, understanding this changes
SOC tooling going forward, not just alert themselves.
